02:16:26 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I believe you 100%! What you experienced is very real, and it brings up the ultimate exception to the rule: **Wool.** If you ironed a wool sweater and it shrank, you didn't just imagine it. You accidentally performed a very specific scientific process called **felting**. Here is exactly why your wool sweater shrank under the iron, even though a cotton button-down shirt wouldn't: ### 1. Wool fibers have "scales" Think back to the hair analogy. Human hair and sheep's wool are very similar, but under a microscope, a wool fiber looks like a pinecone. It is covered in tiny, overlapping scales. When wool gets hot and slightly damp (which is exactly what happens when you use the **steam** setting on an iron), those tiny scales open up and stand on end. ### 2. Sliding the iron = Agitation Remember how we talked about needing "agitation" (tumbling and rubbing) to shrink clothes? When you iron a cotton shirt, you slide the heavy hot metal back and forth. Cotton doesn't mind this. But if you slide an iron back and forth across a steamy wool sweater, that sliding friction acts as agitation. You are rubbing those open "pinecone" scales against each other. Because of the heat, the moisture from the steam, and the rubbing of the iron, those microscopic scales act like little ratchets. They slide past each other and lock together permanently. The fibers tangle up tightly, and the sweater shrinks. ### 3. Sweaters are Knit, not Woven A cotton dress shirt is *woven* on a loom, meaning the threads are packed tightly together in a grid. A sweater is *knit*, meaning it is made of a series of interlocking loops with lots of empty space between them. Because knits have so much empty space, they have a lot more room to collapse and shrink inward when those fibers start locking together. ### How tailors avoid this (Pressing vs. Ironing) Because of exactly what happened to you, professional tailors have a strict rule: **You never *iron* wool, you only *press* it.** * **Ironing** is sliding the hot metal back and forth. (This rubs the wool scales together and shrinks/felts the fabric). * **Pressing** is picking the iron straight up in the air, moving it over, and setting it straight down flat. (This applies heat and pressure to flatten the wrinkle, but without the rubbing friction that causes shrinking). So, your experience is completely valid! By applying heat, steam, and the friction of sliding the iron, you accidentally created the exact "perfect storm" needed to shrink wool. It proves just how sensitive fibers are to exactly *how* heat and movement are applied!
